window.EXPL = {"2014A-p10#a": "<p>We turn each inequality into an equation by adding one non-negative variable that soaks up the gap between the two sides. The \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint may fall short of its bound, so we add a slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint may overshoot, so we subtract a surplus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The objective is unchanged (the new variables get coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). The standard form is:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mrow><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div>\n<p>This is two equations in five non-negative variables, exactly the form simplex needs.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A slack fills the room left under a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" and a surplus removes the excess over a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\", so both become equalities.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#b": "<p>The vertex is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so we set the three non-basic variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Now solve the two equations for the basic variables. From the first equation,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>From the second equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>, so</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Both basic values are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so this is a valid basic feasible solution: the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Fix the non-basic variables at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and the two equations pin down the two basic ones.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#c": "<p>The variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters the basis first.</p>\n<p>We rewrite the objective using only the non-basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Raising a non-basic variable from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> changes the objective at the rate of its coefficient: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The largest positive rate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, belonging to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is chosen to enter the basis.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Write the profit in terms of the variables currently at zero and push the one that pays the most per unit.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#d": "<p>The variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves the basis.</p>\n<p>We raise the entering variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, keeping the other non-basics <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and see which basic variable reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> first. The basic variables are</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> has no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> term it stays at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and imposes no limit. But <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, so the requirement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> (min ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>). Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves, and after the pivot <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> with new basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The entering variable grows only until the first basic variable would go negative, and that one leaves.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#e": "<p>The objective value after the first pivot is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>32</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>From part (c) the objective in non-basic form is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. After the pivot the basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Substituting,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>This matches the intuition: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> climbed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> units at rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></math> to the starting value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The non-basic form already carries the constant, so the new value is one substitution away.</p>\n", "2014A-p4#a": "<p>Introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for each vertex, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is placed in the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. The cost of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which we minimize. Vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is dominated exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> or some neighbor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. when at least one vertex of the closed neighborhood <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is chosen; this is the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The ILP is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> solution picks the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; the constraints say exactly that every vertex is dominated, and the objective is the total weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. So feasible integer solutions correspond exactly to dominating sets and their weights, and the ILP optimum is the minimum-weight dominating set.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each vertex just needs at least one chosen vertex in its closed neighborhood, which is what \"dominated\" means.</p>\n", "2014A-p4#b": "<p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> to be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>-cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> on vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> (edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>), with all weights equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Integer optimum. A single vertex dominates only itself and its two neighbors — three of the four vertices — and misses the opposite vertex, so no one vertex dominates all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Two vertices do suffice (for example the opposite pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> dominates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> dominates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>), so the minimum dominating set has cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Fractional solution. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Each vertex's closed neighborhood sums to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So this point is LP-feasible, with cost</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Thus the LP optimum is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, while every integer solution costs at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the LP optimum lies strictly below the ILP optimum and so cannot be attained at an integral point.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> On a square, integer domination forces two whole vertices, but spreading half-weights around covers everyone for less.</p>\n", "2014A-p5#x": "<p>We reduce the problem to Steiner tree by merging all senders into a single terminal.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Add one artificial vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, and connect <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to every sender <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with an edge of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; call the resulting graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, run the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation algorithm for Steiner tree (seen in the problem sets) with required terminals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Output the returned subgraph, dropping the artificial vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and its zero-cost edges.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, a subgraph connects all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> if and only if every receiver has a path to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>; and since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is attached only to senders, any path from a receiver to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> must use a zero-cost edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so it is exactly a path from that receiver to some sender (and conversely any receiver-to-sender path extends to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> for free). Hence minimum-cost subgraphs connecting every receiver to some sender correspond exactly to minimum-cost Steiner trees for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and because the added edges cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the optimal Steiner-tree cost equals the optimal cost of the original problem.</p>\n<p>The Steiner-tree algorithm returns a solution of cost at most twice the optimal Steiner tree, i.e. at most twice the optimum of our problem; removing the free artificial edges leaves a subgraph of the original graph of the same cost. So this is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation, and it runs in polynomial time as it is a single call to the polynomial Steiner-tree approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Gluing all senders to one free hub turns \"reach some sender\" into \"reach the hub,\" which is exactly a Steiner tree we already know how to 2-approximate.</p>\n", "2014A-p6#x": "<p>Split each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-bit number into a high and low half of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> bits: write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Then</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The middle coefficient can be obtained from just one extra product using the identity</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>so</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Hence to multiply two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-bit numbers we only need three products of (about) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-bit numbers — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — together with a constant number of additions and shifts of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-bit numbers, each costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by the allowed basic operations. Computing the three half-size products recursively, the running time satisfies</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>By the Master theorem with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the recursion dominates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The schoolbook method needs four half-products; one clever combination replaces the two middle ones, cutting four recursive calls down to three.</p>\n", "2014A-p8#a": "<p>Algorithm: set each variable to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently and uniformly at random. We show its expected number of satisfied clauses is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Fix a clause. It has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> literals from distinct variables (no two literals in a clause share a variable), so their truth values are independent, each false with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The clause is unsatisfied only when all three literals are false, which happens with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>; hence it is satisfied with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>For clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is satisfied, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> count the satisfied clauses. Even though clauses may share variables, by linearity of expectation\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSince no assignment can satisfy more than all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. So the algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>-approximation in expectation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A random clause fails only if all three coin flips go wrong (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>), so on average <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> of the clauses are satisfied, and expectations add up even when clauses overlap.</p>\n", "2014A-p8#b": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the number of satisfied clauses, so from part (a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. We want to bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Write the event in terms of the number of unsatisfied clauses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, which is a non-negative random variable with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nBy Markov's inequality applied to the non-negative variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSo the probability that fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> of the clauses are satisfied is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, a closed-form bound.</p>\n<p>(We cannot use a Chernoff bound here because the clauses are not independent, since they can share variables. If one nonetheless assumed independence, Chernoff would give the much smaller bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>168</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, but that assumption is not valid.)</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> On average only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses fail, so by Markov the chance of more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> failing (which is what \"below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>\" means) is at most one half.</p>\n", "2014A-p9#a": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be a maximal matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> a maximum matching; we show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, which is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation. Take any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. At least one endpoint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> must be an endpoint of some edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>: otherwise both endpoints of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> are untouched by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> would still be a matching, contradicting the maximality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Map each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> to one of its endpoints that lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the set of vertices touched by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Because the edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> are pairwise vertex-disjoint, distinct edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> map to distinct vertices, so this map is injective. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges it touches exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> also cannot exceed the maximum, any maximal matching is within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of the maximum matching.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Maximality means every optimal edge is blocked by a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> already uses, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>'s few edges only supply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> such vertices.</p>\n", "2014B-p7#a": "<p>There always exists a packing into at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bins (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is the number of items), so we may use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> candidate bins. Introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> indicating whether bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is open, and a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> indicating whether item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is packed in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. The integer linear program is:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></div>\nsubject to\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>(each&#x000A0;item&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;packed&#x000A0;into&#x000A0;some&#x000A0;bin)</mtext></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>(an&#x000A0;item&#x000A0;can&#x000A0;be&#x000A0;placed&#x000A0;only&#x000A0;in&#x000A0;an&#x000A0;open&#x000A0;bin)</mtext></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>(no&#x000A0;bin&#x000A0;exceeds&#x000A0;capacity&#x000A0;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>)</mtext></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe objective counts the open bins, which we minimize.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One switch per item-bin pair and one per bin; open as few bins as possible while every item lands somewhere and no bin overflows.</p>\n", "2014B-p7#b": "<p>Here the number of bins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is fixed and we want the smallest common bin capacity so all items fit. This is exactly the makespan scheduling problem: think of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> bins as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> machines and each item's weight as a job's processing time; the minimum feasible capacity is the minimum achievable maximum load.</p>\n<p>Algorithm (greedy least-loaded, as proved in pset 2 problem 3):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Go through the items one by one.</li>\n<li>Place each item into the bin that currently has the smallest total load.</li>\n<li>Output the maximum load over all bins in the resulting packing as the required capacity.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correctness / ratio: this greedy assignment was shown in class (pset 2 problem 3) to be a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for minimizing the maximum load, i.e. the maximum load it produces is at most twice the optimal minimum capacity. Reporting that maximum load therefore gives a capacity within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of optimal, and all items fit by construction. So this is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for the minimum-bin-capacity variant.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Minimizing the largest bin load is just makespan scheduling, and always feeding the next item to the emptiest bin is known to stay within twice the best possible.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#a": "<p>This is the minimum vertex cover problem.</p>\n<p>Each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> says whether vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is chosen, so a solution corresponds to a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> holds if and only if at least one of its endpoints is chosen, which is exactly the condition that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> covers every edge. The objective minimizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. So IP1 asks for the smallest set of vertices that touches every edge, which is the minimum vertex cover of the graph.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Picking vertices so every edge has a chosen endpoint, using as few vertices as possible, is exactly vertex cover.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#b": "<p>The relaxation LP1 is obtained by dropping integrality, replacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is redundant here. The objective is a minimization and every constraint on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a lower bound, so no optimal solution ever needs a coordinate above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: if some optimal solution had <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, lowering it to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> still satisfies every edge constraint (an endpoint equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> already makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) and strictly decreases the objective. Hence we may omit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> without changing the optimum.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Relaxing means allowing fractional vertices in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>; since we are minimizing, the algorithm never wants a value above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the upper bound is free.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#c": "<p>Applying the dual rules to LP1 gives one dual variable per primal edge-constraint and one dual constraint per primal vertex-variable. Introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for each edge constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Each primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> has objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and appears in the constraints of exactly the edges incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, giving the dual constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So DLP1 is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>This puts nonnegative weights on the edges so that the total weight at each vertex is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and maximizes the total edge weight — a fractional matching.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Dualizing is a fixed recipe: edges become variables and vertices become \"total incident weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" constraints, giving a fractional matching.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#d": "<p>Making DLP1 integral means adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> marks that edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is picked and a solution is a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. For each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says that at most one picked edge is incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. no two chosen edges share an endpoint. The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> maximizes the number of chosen edges. So the integer program asks for the largest set of pairwise non-adjacent edges, which is the maximum matching problem.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> \"At most one chosen edge per vertex\" is exactly a matching, and maximizing the count gives maximum matching, the natural dual of minimum vertex cover.</p>\n", "2015A-p5#x": "<p>We need roughly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1800</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> samples. We sample points uniformly in the unit cube, count the fraction that land inside the ball, scale it to estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, and use the Chernoff bound to see how many samples force the error below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>&#x00025;</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>For the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th sampled point, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator r.v. that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if the point falls inside the ball of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Since the ball has volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> and the point is uniform in the unit cube, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the number of points inside the ball, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. The algorithm outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> as its estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> estimates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>We want the estimate to miss <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math> by more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>&#x00025;</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>This is a deviation of the average <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> from its mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow></math>. Apply the Chernoff bound for a sum of independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>60</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Now we just require this bound to be at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1800</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>which rearranges to</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1800</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>So taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x02308;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1800</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x02309;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> samples guarantees that with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> the estimate is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>&#x00025;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>. This is the requested closed-form expression, no numeric evaluation needed.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> More samples make the observed fraction hug its true value, and Chernoff tells us exactly how many are enough to pin the error down.</p>\n", "2015A-p6#a": "<p>We prove by induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> that after packing all items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bins are not full.</p>\n<p>Base case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> are the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>'s (each fills a bin by itself) and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>'s (which are packed in pairs). The only way to leave a non-full bin is a single unpaired <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so there is at most one non-full bin, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Inductive step. Assume that after packing all items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins. Now we pack the items of weight exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the algorithm keeps placing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> items into a bin until that bin becomes exactly full, and it never opens a new bin while some bin still has room. So every bin opened during this phase, except possibly the very last one opened, ends up completely full. Therefore this phase adds at most one new non-full bin.</p>\n<p>Combining, the number of non-full bins is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, completing the induction.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Because each weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> evenly, a bin fills perfectly before the next one opens, so each new weight level can leave only one partly-filled bin behind.</p>\n", "2015A-p6#b": "<p>Every bin other than the last one opened holds items of total weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The reason a new bin was opened for the item of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is that this item did not fit into any existing bin. That means every existing bin had less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> of free space at that moment, so each of them was already filled to more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Since this was the last time a new bin was opened, all later items are only added to existing bins, and no bin's load ever decreases. Therefore, in the final packing, every bin except the last-opened one contains items of total weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The algorithm opens a new bin only as a last resort, so the moment it does, every other bin must have been almost completely full.</p>\n", "2015A-p6#c": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> be the number of full bins and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> the number of non-full bins the algorithm produces, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the weight of the item that caused the last bin to be opened. We bound the total weight of all items from below. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> full bins contribute weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. By part (b), each of the other <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins (all except the last-opened one) contributes at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>total&#x000A0;weight</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nBy part (a) we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and therefore the total weight is greater than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Every bin can hold total weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the optimal number of bins satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>total&#x000A0;weight</mtext><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is an integer, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The algorithm uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> bins, so it uses at most one bin more than the optimum. Hence the algorithm is an additive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The items weigh nearly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in total, and since each bin holds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, even a perfect packing needs almost that many bins, leaving the algorithm only one bin behind.</p>\n", "2015A-p7#a": "<p>Add slack variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, one per inequality, to get the starting dictionary (the non-basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> are set to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>):</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>For the first pivot I choose an entering variable with a positive objective coefficient; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has the largest (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters. Now I raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and check which basic variable reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> first: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> row allows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> row has coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> so it never binds, and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> row allows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. The tightest bound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves the basis.</p>\n<p>Solving the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> row for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Substituting this into the other rows gives the dictionary after the first pivot:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>11</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One pivot swaps a zero variable for the constraint that blocks it first, then rewrites everything in the new set of zero variables.</p>\n", "2015A-p7#b": "<p>The primal is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so its dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The three primal constraints give dual variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and their right-hand sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> form the dual objective. Each primal variable gives one dual constraint (read down its column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> its objective coefficient):</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>12</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Taking the dual just transposes the roles: each constraint becomes a variable and each variable becomes a constraint.</p>\n", "2015A-p7#c": "<p>In part (a), after the first pivot, set the non-basic variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow></math>. All variables are non-negative, so this is a feasible solution of the primal maximization LP, and its objective value is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Since the primal is a maximization and we have a feasible point of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>, the primal optimum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>. By the strong duality theorem the dual optimum equals the primal optimum, so the dual optimum is also at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One feasible primal point already gives value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>, and duality passes that bound straight to the dual.</p>\n", "2015A-p7#d": "<p>Introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, and minimize their sum. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the smallest number that is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, I bound each new variable below by its expression and by its negation:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> are free. These constraints force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; since we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, at the optimum each of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is pushed down to exactly equal its absolute value, so the LP has the same optimum as the original problem.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Minimizing a variable kept above both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> squeezes it down to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2015B-p3#x": "<p>We prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The idea is to take an optimal tour, split its edges into two matchings by taking alternate edges, and observe that the cheaper of the two is a matching of weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is no larger than any matching.</p>\n\n<p>Assume without loss of generality that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is even (otherwise a maximum matching cannot cover every vertex, and the two matchings below are still maximum matchings by the same argument). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the vertices of a minimum weight traveling salesperson tour, listed in the order they are visited. The tour uses the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>and their total weight is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Split these edges into two sets by taking alternate edges of the cycle:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Each vertex is an endpoint of exactly one edge in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and exactly one edge in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are (maximum) matchings in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. Together they use each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> tour edges exactly once, so</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Since the two weights sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, the smaller of them is at most the average:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Finally, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the minimum weight over all maximum matchings of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, so it is no larger than either <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Combining the two facts,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>which is exactly what we wanted to prove.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A tour is a cycle, and a cycle falls apart into two matchings by taking every other edge, so one of them must cost at most half the tour.</p>\n", "2015B-p5#x": "<p>Algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> as a black box with a time budget:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps.</li>\n<li>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> terminates within that budget, return its answer.</li>\n<li>Otherwise, halt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Running time: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> never runs more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, so it runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time on every input.</p>\n<p>Correctness: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> terminates, its answer is correct, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is a Las Vegas algorithm that always returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> can only be wrong when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> fails to finish within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> outputs the default <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since the running time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, Markov's inequality gives</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> errs with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. it is correct with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. This bound uses only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so it depends on the algorithm's randomness and not on the particular input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A run much longer than average is rare, so cutting off at ten times the expected time almost always still lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> finish and answer correctly.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#a": "<p>This is the maximum independent set problem. Each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> decides whether vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is chosen, and the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts the chosen vertices, so we are picking as many vertices as possible. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> forces at most one endpoint of each edge to be chosen, so no two chosen vertices are adjacent. A set of vertices with no edge inside it is exactly an independent set, so maximizing the number of chosen vertices is finding a maximum independent set.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Forbidding both endpoints of every edge is the same as saying no two picked vertices are neighbors.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#b": "<p>The LP relaxation keeps the same objective and constraints but drops the integrality requirement, replacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected, every vertex lies on some edge, and each such edge constraint together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> already forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; so no separate upper bound is needed and the fractional values stay in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Relaxing just lets a vertex be picked \"fractionally\" instead of all-or-nothing.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#c": "<p>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. This is feasible: for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Its objective value is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The LP is a maximization, so its optimum is at least the value of any feasible solution. We exhibited a feasible solution of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, hence the LP optimum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Half a unit on every vertex satisfies each edge exactly, and there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> of them.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#d": "<p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the complete graph on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></math> vertices, in which every pair of vertices is joined by an edge. Any independent set can contain at most one vertex: if it contained two vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> would make <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, violating the constraint. A single vertex is trivially independent, so the maximum independent set — the optimum of the ILP in (a) — has size exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> In a clique everyone is everyone's neighbor, so you can keep only one vertex.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#e": "<p>The primal LP from part (b) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. It is in standard maximization form (all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraints, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), so its dual is a minimization with one variable per primal constraint and one constraint per primal variable.</p>\n<p>Introduce a dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. Each edge constraint has right-hand side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Each primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> has objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and appears with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in exactly the edge constraints touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>; this gives one dual constraint per vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>So the dual is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The dual spreads weight over the edges so that every vertex is \"covered\" with total weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — a fractional edge cover, the mirror image of the primal picking vertices.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#f": "<p>Reading the dual with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> decides whether edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is chosen, the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> minimizes the number of chosen edges, and the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is touched by at least one chosen edge. A set of edges that touches every vertex is an edge cover, so this ILP is the minimum edge cover problem.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> \"Every vertex is touched by some chosen edge\" is exactly what an edge cover means.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#g": "<p>Algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Compute a maximum matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, using the polynomial-time matching algorithm from class.</li>\n  <li>For every vertex not covered by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, add one arbitrary edge incident to it (such an edge exists because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected, so there are no isolated vertices). Output the resulting edge set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correctness. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> is an edge cover: a vertex matched by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is covered by its matching edge, and every unmatched vertex is covered by the edge we added for it. Its size is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> covers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices and we add exactly one edge for each of the remaining <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices.</p>\n<p>Now we show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> is optimal. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> be a minimum edge cover. Assign each vertex to one edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> that covers it. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> be the number of edges assigned two vertices and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> the number assigned exactly one; every vertex is assigned to some edge, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>. All <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> edges that received a vertex are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> doubly-assigned edges have disjoint endpoints, so they form a matching, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. Therefore</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> is itself a valid edge cover we also have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and the algorithm returns a minimum edge cover.</p>\n<p>Runtime. The cost is dominated by the maximum matching computation, which is polynomial; adding one edge per unmatched vertex takes linear time. So the whole algorithm runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A matching covers two vertices with one edge, the best an edge can do, so build on a biggest matching and patch up the leftovers one edge each.</p>\n", "2016A-p5#x": "<p>False. The sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> both start empty, and a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> only if it has strictly more neighbors already in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> than in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise it is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Consider the first vertex processed: with both sets empty it has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in each, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is false, so it goes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> empty. As long as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> stays empty, every later vertex has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so it never has strictly more neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> than in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, and it too is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. By induction all vertices end up in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Then no edge crosses the partition, so the algorithm's cut has value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. But take, for example, a graph consisting of a single edge: its maximum cut is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The algorithm outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> while the optimum is positive, so the ratio (optimum over algorithm) is unbounded and no constant factor, in particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, can hold. Hence the algorithm is not a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> starts empty and the rule only moves a vertex there when it already has friends in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, nothing ever enters <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and the cut collapses to nothing.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#a": "<p>We use one binary variable per edge and cap the degree at each vertex. For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means the edge is in the matching. We want the heaviest matching, and a matching is a set of edges using each vertex at most once, which is a linear inequality at every vertex. The program is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector touches each vertex at most once, so it is exactly a matching, and its objective value is that matching's total weight; an optimal solution is a maximum-weight matching.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Put an on/off switch on each edge and forbid any vertex from being used twice.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#b": "<p>The LP relaxation from (a) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. It is a maximization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints, so its dual is a minimization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints: one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per vertex constraint, and one dual constraint per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, that is, per edge.</p>\n<p>The edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> appears only in the two vertex constraints of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, each with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and its objective coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so its dual constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The dual is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each vertex constraint of the primal turns into a dual variable, and each edge turns into a dual constraint.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#c": "<p>The LP in (b) is a (fractional) weighted vertex cover. Read each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as an amount of weight (resource) placed on vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> says that for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the weight sitting on its two endpoints must total at least its weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> asks to do this using as little total weight as possible.</p>\n<p>In words: put enough weight on the vertices so that for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> the amount on its two endpoints is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, while minimizing the total weight used. It is fractional because we are in the LP relaxation rather than the integer program.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Cover every edge's weight using the cheapest total load spread over its endpoints.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#d": "<p>We bring the LP to the equality (dictionary) form simplex runs on by adding one nonnegative variable to each inequality.</p>\n<p>The \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> loses a surplus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> gains a slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>So the equality form is, with all variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Solving each equation for its slack gives the dictionary form simplex starts from:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Simplex only works on equalities, so each inequality trades its gap for one nonnegative slack variable.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#e": "<p>The vertex is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so the nonbasic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Substituting into the two equality constraints, each collapses to a single unknown.</p>\n<p>From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Both basic values are nonnegative, so this is a feasible vertex: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A basis just means setting the other variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and solving the equations for the ones that remain.</p>\n", "2017A-p1#a": "<p>The running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. I set up the recurrence from the costs given and apply the master theorem.</p>\n<p>Each call splits the input into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> subproblems of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. The work done outside the recursive calls is the divide step, which takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, plus the combine step, which takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> grows faster than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the total non-recursive work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This gives</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>To apply the master theorem I compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. We have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.79</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is polynomially larger than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: concretely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for a small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The regularity condition also holds, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. We are therefore in the third case of the master theorem, where the top-level work dominates, and</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The base case (problems of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></math> solved directly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>500</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time) applies only to inputs of bounded size and so does not affect the asymptotic running time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The subproblems shrink so fast that all of them together cost less than the single linear pass at the top, so that top pass sets the running time.</p>\n", "2017A-p1#b": "<p>The running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. I write the recurrence, then unfold it and sum the resulting geometric series.</p>\n<p>Each call does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work outside the recursion (the divide step plus the combine step, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>), and then makes one recursive call on a matrix of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math>-by-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math>. Writing the per-call cost as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Now I unfold the recursion. Going one level deeper halves both dimensions, so the added cost at the next level is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the level after that contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and so on. Summing the costs over all levels gives</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The series in parentheses is a geometric sum with ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so it is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>It is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, since the very first call already does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work. The recursion stops once the matrix has a single row (a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>-by-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> matrix handled directly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time), which is consistent with this bound. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The work halves at every deeper level, so the whole recursion costs only a constant factor more than the first call.</p>\n", "2017A-p1#c": "<p>True. This is exactly linearity of expectation, which holds for any random variables whether or not they are independent.</p>\n<p>For any two random variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> on the same sample space and any constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. This follows directly from the definition of expectation as a sum over outcomes weighted by their probabilities: summation is linear, so constants pull out and the expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations. Nowhere does this use independence.</p>\n<p>Applying it with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>So the statement is always true, even though <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> may be dependent. (Independence would only be needed for a product such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, never for a linear combination.)</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Averages add up no matter how the variables are related; dependence would only matter for products or variances, not for a plain sum.</p>\n", "2017A-p1#e": "<p>True. The algorithm meets both requirements in the definition of a PTAS.</p>\n<p>A PTAS for a minimization problem <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math> is a family of algorithms such that, for every fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, there is an algorithm that returns a solution of quality at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times the optimum, and runs in time that is polynomial in the input size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> is treated as a constant. The running time is allowed to depend badly on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>; only its dependence on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> must be polynomial.</p>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> already produces, for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a solution whose quality is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times optimal, so the approximation guarantee is exactly what a PTAS asks for.</p>\n<p>For the running time, fix any constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> is a constant, so the exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> is a constant, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is a fixed polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. For example <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the exponent grows as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> shrinks, but for each fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> it stays constant. This is precisely the behavior a PTAS permits.</p>\n<p>Both conditions hold, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is a PTAS for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>. (It is not an FPTAS, which would additionally require the running time to be polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> as well, but that was not asked.)</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A PTAS only needs the running time to be polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> is fixed, and a constant exponent like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math> is exactly that.</p>\n", "2017A-p3#x": "<p>True: the described algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for metric Path-TSP. We show the Euler path exists, bound the tree cost by OPT, bound the matching cost by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> using two candidate matchings, and then combine and shortcut.</p>\n<p>Euler path exists. The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is not empty, since every leaf of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, hence is odd. Adding the matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> flips the parity of every matched vertex, so in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> the only vertices that remain odd are the two unmatched vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. A connected graph with exactly two odd-degree vertices has an Euler path, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> has one.</p>\n<p>Tree cost. OPT is a spanning path, which in particular is a spanning tree, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is a minimum spanning tree, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Matching cost. We proved in class that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is even; write its vertices as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the order they appear along OPT. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Each of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves exactly two vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> unmatched, so each is a valid candidate for the kind of matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> the algorithm computes. Now OPT contains the disjoint subpaths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and by the triangle inequality each matching edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math> costs at most the OPT-subpath between them. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> together use each of these subpaths once, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is a minimum-cost such matching, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Combine. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Shortcutting repeated vertices in the Euler path only removes edges and, by the triangle inequality, never increases cost, so the resulting Hamilton path costs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. The algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Walking the odd vertices in OPT's order splits OPT into subpaths that two alternating matchings cover once each, so the cheaper one costs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2017A-p4#x": "<p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> independent samples, output the sample mean as the estimate, and use a Chernoff bound to fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn></mrow></math> samples suffice, independent of the stream length.</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for whether the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th sampled bit is a one, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> be the true fraction of ones. The samples are independent with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>, and the estimate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>By the Chernoff bound (both tails, hence the factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>),\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nWe want this to be at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Taking logarithms, it suffices that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, i.e.\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>30000</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> always, taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30000</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn></mrow></math> works for every possible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>90000</mn></mrow></math> samples guarantee the estimate is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> of the true frequency with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>; note this is a fixed constant, independent of the sequence length.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Chernoff sets the number of samples needed for accuracy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> at confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> lets us drop the unknown <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2017A-p6#a": "<p>We use one binary variable per element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is chosen for the hitting set and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not. The chosen set is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The condition that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is a hitting set is that it meets every subset in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>: for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> we need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. at least one element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is chosen. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts how many elements of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> are chosen, \"at least one\" is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>We want a hitting set of smallest cardinality, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so we minimize this sum. The program is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> requirement forces each element to be either fully in or fully out, so feasible solutions are exactly the hitting sets and an optimal solution is a minimum hitting set.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One yes/no switch per element, and \"hit every set\" just means each set has at least one switch turned on.</p>", "2017A-p6#b": "<p>Start from the LP relaxation of the program in (a): replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The upper bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> are redundant here, because we minimize and every constraint has nonnegative coefficients, so no optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> ever needs to exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; we drop them and keep only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The relaxed primal is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Now take the dual. There is one primal constraint for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, so we introduce one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Since the primal is a minimization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and nonnegative variables, the dual is a maximization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and nonnegative variables.</p>\n<p>The dual objective uses the primal right-hand sides, which are all equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The dual constraint for primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> reads off the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> across the primal constraints: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears (with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) in the constraint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, and the primal objective coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The dual is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One weight per subset, maximizing total weight while no single element is loaded past one.</p>", "2017A-p6#c": "<p>Take the dual from (b) and make it a binary integer program by requiring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Now read off what each piece means. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means we choose the subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; let the chosen collection be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts how many subsets we choose, and we maximize that count. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says that for every element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, at most one chosen subset contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>; equivalently, no two chosen subsets share an element, so the chosen subsets are pairwise disjoint.</p>\n<p>So the combinatorial problem is: find a maximum-size collection of subsets from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> that are mutually (pairwise) disjoint.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Grab as many of the given subsets as possible without ever reusing an element.</p>", "2017A-p6#d": "<p>Standard form here means a maximization in which every constraint is a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" inequality and every variable is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. We repair each part of the given LP that breaks one of these requirements.</p>\n<p>First fix the variable signs. We have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is already fine. Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so the new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Under this substitution the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Next handle the equality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. An equality is the same as the two inequalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: the first becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>; the second becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to make it \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Finally the inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> is already \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\"; applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> turns it into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Collecting everything, the standard form is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Flip the negative variables, split the equality into two one-sided bounds, and point every inequality the same way.</p>", "2017B-p2#a": "<p>The greedy algorithm sorts items by the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and adds them in descending order while they fit. We show its approximation ratio is unbounded by exhibiting an instance where greedy does arbitrarily worse than the optimum.</p>\n<p>Take two items and capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> (large): item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Their ratios are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so greedy considers item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> first and takes it. After taking item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the remaining capacity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which is less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, so item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> no longer fits and greedy stops with total value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The optimum instead takes item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> alone, which fits since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, for value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Thus\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhich grows without bound as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence the greedy algorithm's approximation ratio is not bounded.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Greedy grabs one tiny high-ratio item and then blocks itself from the huge item that was the real prize.</p>\n", "2017B-p2#b": "<p>For each item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is chosen and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is left out. We want maximum total cost subject to total weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe objective sums the cost of the chosen items, the constraint keeps their total weight within the capacity, and the integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> forces each item to be either fully in or fully out.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One 0/1 switch per item: pack it or not, maximize cost without busting the weight budget.</p>\n", "2017B-p2#c": "<p>The relaxation keeps the same objective and weight constraint but replaces each integrality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, allowing fractional items:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThis is now a linear program, since every constraint and the objective are linear.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Drop the \"whole item only\" rule and let items be taken in fractions, turning the integer program into a solvable LP.</p>\n", "2017B-p2#d": "<p>The primal is a maximization LP with constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. We assign a dual variable to each primal constraint: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for the capacity constraint and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for each constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The dual minimizes over the constraint right-hand sides, and for each primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> it has one constraint collecting the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> (namely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the capacity row and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th row), which must be at least the objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>W</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One price <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> per unit of capacity plus a price <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> per item, set just high enough that every item's value is covered.</p>\n", "2017B-p3#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be the random variable counting the number of steps a random walk started at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> takes to first reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. Since a walk that has visited all vertices has in particular visited <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, the time to reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is at most the cover time, so by the class bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> on the cover time we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, Markov's inequality gives\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nTherefore the walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability at least\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The walk expects to hit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, so giving it four times that budget makes overshooting unlikely (at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>) by Markov.</p>\n", "2017B-p4#a": "<p>Consider any assignment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> together with its bitwise negation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math>, which assigns to each variable the opposite of its value in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. We claim every clause is satisfied by at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math>. Indeed, if a clause is not satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, then all three of its literals are false under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>; flipping every variable turns all three literals true, so the clause is satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math>. Hence no clause is failed by both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Summing over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses, the number satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> plus the number satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, since each clause contributes at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to this sum. Two nonnegative numbers adding to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> cannot both be below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> clauses. Therefore an assignment satisfying at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> clauses always exists.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> An assignment and its exact opposite together cover every clause, so one of the two must handle at least half of them.</p>\n", "2018A-p1#a": "<p>Strassen makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> recursive multiplications of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices, plus a constant number of additions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices, each addition costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So the running time satisfies</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Apply the Master theorem with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and combine cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.807</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, the recursion (the leaves) dominates, and</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Seven subproblems that only halve in size grow faster than the quadratic merge, so the bottom of the recursion tree sets the cost.</p>\n", "2018A-p1#b": "<p>The running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The recurrence is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>where the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the split cost (it dominates the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> combine). Look at the work level by level in the recursion tree. The top level does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work. The next level does</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>36</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>which is a constant factor less than the level above. The same shrinking happens at every level, so the per-level work forms a decreasing geometric series and the total is a constant times the top level. Therefore</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each level does a fixed fraction less work than the one above, so the very first level's <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> already sets the total.</p>\n", "2018A-p1#e": "<p>Monte Carlo. Karger's randomized min-cut algorithm always runs in a fixed (polynomial) number of steps, but it may return a cut that is not minimum — it outputs a minimum cut only with some probability. An algorithm whose running time is fixed but whose answer can be wrong is Monte Carlo. (A Las Vegas algorithm is the other way around: always correct, but with random running time.)</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> It can be wrong but is never slow, and that trade-off is exactly what \"Monte Carlo\" means.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#a": "<p>The algorithm sweeps the subsets in any order, and whenever it meets a subset not yet hit by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, it adds all of that subset's (at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>) elements to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Initialize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>For each subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, in arbitrary order: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02190;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise do nothing.</li>\n  <li>Return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This returns a hitting set: after we process a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, we always have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, because either it was already hit before we reached it, or we just added all of its elements to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>It runs in polynomial time: there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> subsets, and for each we test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> and possibly add at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> elements, which is cheap. (A key property for the analysis in part (b): we add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> only when it currently shares nothing with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, so the fully-added subsets are pairwise disjoint.)</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Grab an entire unhit subset at once; that both guarantees coverage and keeps the grabbed subsets disjoint.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#b": "<p>We show the greedy runs in polynomial time, always outputs a hitting set, and returns a set of size at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. The proof is like the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for vertex cover from class.</p>\n<p>Polynomial time: represent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> as a linked list. Testing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mo>&#x022C3;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is bounded by the input size. Since there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> iterations, the total time is polynomial in the input size.</p>\n<p>It is a hitting set: by the rule in the loop, right after processing any subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, so every subset is hit at the end.</p>\n<p>Approximation ratio: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of subsets that triggered an add (those with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> when reached). Each add inserts at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> elements, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. On the other hand, these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> added subsets are pairwise disjoint (we add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> only when it meets nothing already in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>), so no single element belongs to two of them. Any hitting set, and in particular OPT, must contain at least one element from each of these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> disjoint subsets, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Combining, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, which proves the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The disjoint grabbed subsets force OPT to spend at least one element on each, and we spent at most three.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#c": "<p>We run the exact same greedy from part (a), processing each subset when it arrives:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Keep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, initially empty.</li>\n  <li>When a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> arrives: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, add all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise leave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> unchanged.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is a valid online algorithm: it never looks at future subsets and never removes an element once added. After each arriving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is a hitting set for everything seen so far.</p>\n<p>It is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-competitive: the offline analysis in part (b) allowed the subsets to be processed in an arbitrary order and never used any lookahead. So let that arbitrary order be the arrival order. The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> we build online is exactly what the offline greedy would build on the same sequence, so the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> carries over and the algorithm is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-competitive.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The offline greedy already works in any order and never undoes a choice, so the arrival order is just one more valid order.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#a": "<p>We write minimum-cost perfect matching with one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable per edge that says whether the edge is chosen, minimize the total weight of chosen edges, and force every vertex to be covered exactly once.</p>\n<p>Variables: for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is in the matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not.</p>\n<p>The integer linear program is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the total cost of the chosen edges. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says exactly one chosen edge touches each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>; using equality (not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) is what forbids leaving a vertex uncovered, so it enforces a perfect matching. Thus every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is the indicator of a perfect matching, and minimizing the objective gives a minimum-cost one.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One yes/no choice per edge, with \"exactly one chosen edge at each vertex\" pinning down a perfect matching.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#b": "<p>The LP relaxation replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and we may drop the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> because the vertex equalities already imply them:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>To relax, we swap integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, keeping the vertex equalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The upper bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> are redundant. Take an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. The equality at endpoint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and it contains the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. All the other terms are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> follows automatically from the equality together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Since every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is already implied, we remove them, leaving only the vertex equalities and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> An edge value cannot beat the sum at its endpoint, which equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the upper bound comes for free.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#c": "<p>The dual has one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> per vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> (one per primal constraint), and reads\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>free</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Dual variables: each primal constraint is the vertex equality at some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so we introduce one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Because the primal constraint is an equality, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unconstrained in sign (free).</p>\n<p>Objective: the primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and since the primal is a minimization the dual is a maximization.</p>\n<p>Constraints, one per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>: edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in exactly the two endpoint equalities of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, and its objective coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A primal minimization with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> yields a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> dual constraint, namely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Give each vertex a potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>; every edge caps the sum of its two endpoints' potentials by its weight, and we maximize the total.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#d": "<p>We add an auxiliary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> that bounds the absolute value from above by two linear inequalities, and minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The idea is that for any number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> satisfying both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> together say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> then drives it down until one of the two inequalities is tight, so at the optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. No separate sign constraint on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is needed, since the two inequalities already force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The original constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is already linear, so it stays unchanged. Everything is now linear in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, and the optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> An absolute value in a minimization becomes a new variable squeezed above both branches, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2018B-p2#a": "<p>False. Ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> would mean the greedy always returns a maximum independent set, so one family of bad graphs refutes it.</p>\n<p>For any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, with edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> (so the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s form a clique). The degrees are</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is the unique minimum-degree vertex, so the algorithm grabs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> first and deletes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> together with all its neighbors, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s. Only the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>-clique remains; it picks one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which deletes the whole clique. The graph is now empty, so the algorithm stops with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>But the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s have no edges among themselves, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is an independent set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Thus the algorithm returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> was available, and the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> grows without bound, so ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> cannot hold.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Chasing the single smallest-degree vertex drags in neighbors whose removal destroys a large independent set sitting right there.</p>\n", "2018B-p2#b": "<p>True. We show the output is an independent set, bound its size from below, and compare to the trivial upper bound on the optimum.</p>\n<p>Output is independent: whenever a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, all of its neighbors are removed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, so no later pick can be adjacent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> has no internal edges.</p>\n<p>Trivial ceiling: an independent set is a subset of vertices, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Lower bound on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of iterations. Each iteration adds exactly one vertex to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and removes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> together with its neighbors, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math> vertices. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> and the total removed is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The algorithm stops only when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is empty, i.e. all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices are removed, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, giving</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> of optimal. Running time is polynomial: there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> iterations, and storing the vertex degrees in a heap lets us find the minimum-degree vertex and update after each deletion in polynomial time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each vertex you take knocks out at most its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math> neighbors plus itself, so you can never fall more than a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> behind.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#a": "<p>Yes. The randomness in the algorithm comes only from the pivots it chooses, not from the input. For a fixed input array <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, we analyze <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> where the expectation is taken over the algorithm's own random pivot choices. This holds no matter what <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is, including the worst-case array an adversary could hand us, since the array is fixed and only the pivots are random. So the expected-time bound applies to every input, the worst case included.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The average is over the coin flips of the algorithm, so no bad input can spoil it.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#b": "<p>The expectation is over the random choices of pivots made by the algorithm. The input array is fixed; the only source of randomness is which pivot the algorithm picks at each step, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> averages over those choices.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The algorithm's coin flips are the only random thing, so that is what we average over.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#c": "<p>The pivot is chosen uniformly at random, so for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the algorithm picks the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th largest element as the pivot with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. After partitioning (which costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>), it recurses on one side; in the worst case it recurses on the larger of the two parts, which when the pivot is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th largest has size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Conditioning on the pivot and taking expectations,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>max</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nAs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> runs over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> always lies between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and each such index appears at most twice in the sum. Hence we can bound the sum by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> times the terms with index from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhich is the claimed recurrence.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Every pivot leaves a \"larger half\" of size between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and each such size is hit at most twice, giving the factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#d": "<p>We prove by induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> chosen below; this gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> be the constant hidden in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> partition cost, so that term is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Base case: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> be a constant with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. If we choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the base case holds.</p>\n<p>Inductive assumption: assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Inductive step: using the recurrence from part (c) and then the assumption on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is an arithmetic series; by the given fact it equals its first plus last term times half the number of terms, which is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Therefore\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhere the last inequality holds as long as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>So choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> makes every step go through, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Recursing on halves of size at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> only pulls in about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of the work, leaving room for the linear partition cost, so the total stays linear.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#e": "<p>We turn the Monte Carlo algorithm (whose expected number of operations is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>) into a Las Vegas style algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Run the selection algorithm, but keep a counter of the operations performed and abort the run as soon as it reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations.</li>\n<li>Repeat this capped run up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> times.</li>\n<li>If some run finishes within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations, return its answer. If all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> runs are aborted, return the first element of the array.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Operation count: each run performs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations, and we do at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> runs, so at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations inside the runs. Maintaining the counter and controlling the runs adds only a constant factor, and the whole thing can be arranged so the total does not exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations. So the algorithm always performs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations.</p>\n<p>Correctness probability: a run gives the correct answer whenever it finishes within its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> budget (a completed selection run is always correct). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the running time of a single run, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By Markov's inequality,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> runs are independent, so the probability that all of them exceed the budget (the only way we fail) is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Hence the algorithm returns the correct answer with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Cap each run so it can't run long, and since each run beats the cap at least half the time, five independent tries almost never all fail.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#1": "<p>We give each student one yes/no variable, maximize how many students are chosen, and add one capacity line per course. For each student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is selected. We want the largest selected group, so we maximize the count <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. For each course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, the selected students who take it are exactly those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and the rule \"at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> of them\" is the linear constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The full program is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>A student enrolled in several courses appears in several sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so selecting them uses one unit of each of those caps at once.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One yes/no switch per student, count the switches that are on, and cap how many \"on\" students each course may hold.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#2": "<p>We build the \"who attends what\" matrix, write the primal in matrix form, then transpose it to read off the dual. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> membership matrix with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> (row = student, column = course):</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the number of selected attendees of course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the primal is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Transposing back gives the dual, with one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> per course, cost vector equal to the caps, and one \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" row per student:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>M</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mover><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Each of the five rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mover><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> is one student's requirement; for instance row 1 reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Encode membership as a matrix and the dual is mechanical: transpose it and flip Max<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> into Min<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#3": "<p>We transpose the general primal from part 1: one dual variable per course and one dual constraint per student. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for each course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is chosen; the cost of choosing it is the primal cap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The primal's right-hand sides become the dual objective and the direction flips, so we minimize the total cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Each student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives one row: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> sits in exactly the courses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the row is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, forcing every student into at least one chosen course. The full dual is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>In words: choose courses of least total cost so that every student is covered by at least one chosen course. This is a weighted set cover where the courses are the sets, the students are the elements, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the price of using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Duality turns the students' packing problem into a covering problem over courses: buy the cheapest courses that reach everyone.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#4": "<p>False. We give an instance where the rounding \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" produces an infeasible integral solution.</p>\n<p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The only constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The optimal fractional solution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; it is optimal because the constraint holds the sum at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The rounding threshold is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so both round up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, violating the cap, so the rounded solution is infeasible.</p>\n<p>A less trivial example: take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Setting every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> is feasible (each constraint sums <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and it is optimal since any higher value would force three variables summing above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Again the threshold is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> rounds to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and each course's sum becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, infeasible.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A fair fractional split lands every variable exactly on the rounding line, so rounding them all up overfills each cap-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> course.</p>\n", "2021A-p3#1": "<p>The algorithm computes a minimum cycle cover and then stitches its cycles into one tour:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Compute a minimum-weight cycle cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> (the given first step; this runs in polynomial time, and a cover exists because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is complete). Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> in any order, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is its closing edge.</li>\n<li>For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, delete the closing edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and instead add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: rather than closing cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, continue into the first vertex of cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For the last cycle, replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, returning to the first vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and closing the whole thing into one loop.</li>\n<li>Return the resulting tour.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every replacement edge exists because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is complete, and chaining <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves a single cycle through all vertices exactly once, which is a valid TSP tour.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Reroute each cycle's last edge into the next cycle, stitching the small cycles into one big loop.</p>\n", "2021A-p3#2": "<p>We show the output is a valid tour of cost at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>First, it is a tour: merging the cycles as in part (1) produces one cycle that visits every vertex exactly once, so the output is a legal TSP tour.</p>\n<p>Next, there are few cycles. Every cycle has at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices and the cycles are disjoint over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices, so their number is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Each merge is cheap. It replaces an edge of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (weights are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>) by an edge of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, an increase of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and we do this once per cycle, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> times. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Two lower bounds on OPT finish it. An optimal TSP tour is itself a cycle cover (a single cycle through all vertices), so the minimum cover is no heavier: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. Also a tour has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges each of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Combining,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The cycle cover is a lower bound on OPT, and merging adds only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, which is itself at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#1": "<p>We use one binary variable per edge and cap each vertex's degree, with the only twist being that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices may take two edges instead of one. For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is selected. The objective maximizes the number of selected edges, and there is one constraint per vertex: each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex may touch at most one selected edge, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex at most two. The program is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector respects both caps, so it is a semi-matching, and the objective counts its edges; an optimal solution is a maximum semi-matching.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One on/off switch per edge, and each side of the graph gets its own degree cap.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#2": "<p>The graph has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>. We build the constraint matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with one row per vertex and one column per edge, putting a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> where the edge touches the vertex; the right-hand side is each vertex's degree cap (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>):</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>e</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Primal:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The dual swaps the pieces: the cost vector becomes the primal right-hand side, the matrix transposes, and the direction flips. Writing the dual variables as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Rows are vertices and columns are edges; the dual just transposes that incidence matrix.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#3": "<p>The general dual is weighted vertex cover where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices have weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices have weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. There is one binary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> per vertex, indicating whether it is chosen for the cover. For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> there is one constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, forcing at least one endpoint to be selected. The objective minimizes the total weight of selected vertices, and the weights are the caps carried over from the primal: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The program is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>So the dual asks for the cheapest set of vertices touching every edge, charging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Cover every edge with an endpoint, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices cost twice as much as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#4": "<p>False. To disprove a \"for all inputs\" claim, we give one graph where rounding overloads a vertex. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, a star with four edges all meeting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all four edges. This is feasible: each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex carries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> carries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Its value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and it is optimal because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>'s constraint caps the total at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So this is an optimal fractional solution.</p>\n<p>Now round: every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so all four edges are set to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which violates its cap, so the rounded vector is not a semi-matching. Hence the rounding rule does not always produce a feasible integer solution.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Many edges each sitting at exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> all round up together and blow past the degree cap.</p>\n", "2021B-p3#1": "<p>We build a partition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> into three sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> (to be colored <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>), placing the vertices one at a time so that each vertex adds as little illegal weight as possible.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Initialize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Go over the vertices in an arbitrary order. For the current vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and each set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the total weight of edges connecting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to vertices already placed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is minimal.</li>\n<li>After all vertices are placed, output the coloring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An edge is colored legally exactly when its endpoints are in different sets. When we place <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the only edges it colors illegally are those to already-placed vertices of the same set, whose weight is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; by choosing the set of minimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> we add the least possible illegal weight at each step. The algorithm runs in polynomial time, since each vertex is handled once and we only sum edge weights from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to the three groups.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Color the vertices one by one, each time joining the color group it shares the least edge weight with.</p>\n", "2021B-p3#2": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be the vertices in the order the algorithm considers them, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> at the moment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is placed (so they contain only earlier vertices). When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is added, it colors illegally exactly the edges to already-placed vertices of the set it joins; since the algorithm sends <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> to the set minimizing this weight, and the minimum of three numbers is at most their average, the illegal weight created at this step is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is counted exactly once over the whole run — at the moment the later endpoint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is placed, when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is already present. Summing over all vertices,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nthe total weight of all edges. Hence the total weight of illegally colored edges is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Therefore the weight colored legally is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Finally, no coloring can beat the total edge weight, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the algorithm's legal weight is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. This is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each vertex dumps at most a third of its back-edge weight into color clashes, so at least two thirds of all edge weight is colored legally.</p>\n", "2023A-p1#a": "<p>False. In the simplex algorithm as presented in class, the algorithm moves to a different vertex only when it can strictly improve (decrease) the objective value. So a move to a different vertex always strictly changes the objective, and it never moves to a different vertex whose value equals the current one.</p>\n<p>The only place equal values appear is degeneracy: when the current basic feasible solution is degenerate, a pivot step may change the basis while the actual point stays the same. But that is a change of basis at the same vertex, not a move to a different vertex. Hence the described behavior — moving to a different vertex with the same objective value — does not happen, and the statement is false.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A real move to a new vertex always strictly improves the objective; equal-value steps are just basis swaps that stay at the same point.</p>\n", "2023A-p1#b": "<p>True. The isolation lemma does not really use that the weights come from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; it only uses that we draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct weights and that the weight of a set is the sum of its elements' weights. So we reduce the product version back to the usual sum version by taking logarithms.</p>\n<p>Given a multiplicative weight function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>, define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The map <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a bijection, order-preserving, from weight functions into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> onto weight functions into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so it still gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct weights. Moreover it turns products into sums: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Now apply the original (additive) isolation lemma to the weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>: with high probability there is a unique set of minimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>-weight. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> is monotone, that same set is also the unique set of minimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>-weight under the product definition. Hence the isolation lemma still holds for product weights.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Taking logs turns the product weight into an ordinary sum, so the usual isolation lemma applies unchanged.</p>\n", "2023A-p1#c": "<p>True. The inclusion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is immediate: an algorithm whose error is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> certainly has error below the weaker thresholds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>For the other direction, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so there is a randomized algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> independently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times on the same input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, each run with fresh random coins, and accept if and only if the number of accepting runs is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of the runs (the midpoint between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>).</p>\n<p>In both cases the true acceptance probability is at distance at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math> from the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>, so by the Chernoff bound the empirical fraction of accepting runs lands on the wrong side of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math> only with probability that shrinks fast in the number of repetitions — below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> once we use enough runs. Hence the boosted algorithm decides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with error at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, placing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Both inclusions hold, so the two classes are equal.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A fixed gap between the yes- and no-acceptance probabilities can be blown up to near certainty just by voting over many independent runs.</p>\n", "2023A-p1#d": "<p>True. Strassen's algorithm multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices using only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> multiplications (instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>). We use it twice, nested.</p>\n<p>Split each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrix into four <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks, so it becomes a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrix whose entries are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks. Multiply these two block matrices with Strassen at the block level: this uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> block multiplications (the block additions cost no scalar multiplications). Each of those block multiplications is itself a product of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices, which we again do by Strassen with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> scalar multiplications.</p>\n<p>In total the number of scalar multiplications is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn></mrow></math> multiplications suffice.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Nest Strassen once: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> block products, each an inner <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> product costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2023A-p2#x": "<p>Use one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is chosen into the dominating set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not. The size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which we minimize. For a dominating set, every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> must be in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> or have a neighbor in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; this is captured by requiring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The IP is:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>minimize</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Every feasible solution corresponds to a solution of the original problem: given a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> assignment, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the constraint forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for some neighbor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> or has a neighbor in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is a dominating set. Conversely, any dominating set gives a feasible assignment with the same value. So minimizing the objective yields a minimum dominating set.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One yes/no choice per vertex, and each vertex demands that it or a neighbor said yes.</p>\n", "2023A-p3#x": "<p>We first give a randomized algorithm that is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation in expectation, then derandomize it.</p>\n<p>Randomized algorithm: assign each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> independently to one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, each with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Fix an edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. It crosses the cut when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> land in different sets. Whatever set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is assigned to, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> lands in that same set with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> crosses the cut with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. For edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> crosses the cut, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. By linearity of expectation the number of crossing edges has expectation\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSince the optimum can cut at most all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation in expectation.</p>\n<p>Derandomization (conditional expectations, as seen in class): process the vertices one at a time. For the current vertex, try each of the three sets and compute the conditional expectation of the number of crossing edges given the assignments already fixed plus this tentative choice (this is a simple count that can be evaluated efficiently), and permanently place the vertex in the set that gives the largest conditional expectation. Each step can only keep the conditional expectation from dropping, so it never falls below the initial value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. When all vertices are placed, the value equals the actual number of crossing edges, which is therefore at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. This deterministic algorithm runs in polynomial time and achieves a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> With three bins a random edge stays inside its bin only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> of the time, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> of the edges cross on average, and greedily keeping that average up makes it deterministic.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#a": "<p>We relax the integer program by dropping the integrality constraint, and then put the resulting LP into standard form (a minimization with equality constraints and nonnegative variables). Two changes are needed. First, turn the maximization into a minimization by negating the objective: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>. Second, turn each inequality into an equality by adding a nonnegative slack variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. This gives</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> just measures how far vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is from being fully matched. More succinctly, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> incidence matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> (column <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> elsewhere). Then the LP is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> has length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> has length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Negate the objective and let one slack per vertex soak up the unused capacity, and the matching LP is already in the shape simplex wants.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#b": "<p>The primal from part (a) has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> equality constraints, so the dual has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> variables, a free vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Applying the standard dual rule to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>s</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the dual is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Splitting the constraint by column-block, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-columns give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> and the slack columns give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. To simplify, change variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>: the objective becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The clean dual is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>which per edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The slack columns are just the identity, so their dual constraints simply pin the sign of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, and one sign flip cleans everything up.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#c": "<p>Adding the integrality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to the dual LP from part (b) gives the minimum vertex cover problem. Read <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as \"put vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> into the set\" and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> says that for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values this forces at least one endpoint of each edge into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> touches every edge, which is exactly the definition of a vertex cover. The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> asks for the smallest such set, so this is minimum vertex cover.</p>\n<p>In terms of duality: the primal LP was fractional maximum matching and this dual LP is fractional minimum vertex cover, so LP duality gives (max fractional matching) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> (min fractional vertex cover). For bipartite graphs the optima of both LPs turn out to be integral, so maximum matching equals minimum vertex cover, which is Konig's theorem.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Forcing the dual variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> turns \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on every edge\" into \"pick an endpoint of every edge,\" which is precisely covering the graph with vertices.</p>\n", "2023A-p5#x": "<p>The algorithm returns the maximum achievable rank, runs in polynomial time, and errs with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. The idea is to replace every star by a random integer, compute the ordinary rank of the resulting numeric matrix, and argue with the Schwartz&ndash;Zippel lemma that this random substitution almost surely realizes the best possible rank.</p>\n\n<p>The algorithm is the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>For each entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> marked by a star, replace the star by an integer chosen uniformly and independently at random from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compute the rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> of the resulting numeric matrix (by Gaussian elimination, or the parallel rank algorithm from class).</li>\n<li>Return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>The algorithm is efficient because the rank of a numeric matrix can be computed in polynomial time, and drawing the random integers takes time proportional to the number of stars.</p>\n\n<p>We now prove correctness, i.e. that the returned value equals the true maximum with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of stars in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. For a vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the matrix obtained by substituting the entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> for the stars. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>rank</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the maximum achievable rank, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> be an assignment that attains it, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>rank</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, it contains an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> submatrix of rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>: there are subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> such that the submatrix on the rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and hence a nonzero determinant.</p>\n\n<p>Now treat the stars as variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The determinant of the submatrix on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> is then a polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Because a determinant is a sum of products of entries with one entry from each row and column, each variable appears to degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in any term, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Moreover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is not identically zero: substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> makes it equal to the nonzero determinant found above.</p>\n\n<p>By the Schwartz&ndash;Zippel lemma, a nonzero polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> evaluated at a point whose coordinates are chosen uniformly and independently from a finite set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> vanishes with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Therefore, when we pick the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> starred entries independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the determinant of this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> submatrix is nonzero with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, and in that case the random matrix has an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> submatrix with nonzero determinant, so its rank is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>. On the other hand its rank can never exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is by definition the maximum rank achievable by any substitution for the stars. Hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> the rank of the random matrix equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and the algorithm returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>. The error probability is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, and the running time is polynomial.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The best rank is witnessed by some submatrix determinant being nonzero, and that determinant is a low-degree polynomial in the stars, so a random plug-in almost never lands on one of its rare roots.</p>\n", "2023A-p6#x": "<p>Associate with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> the polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> and with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> the polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></math>; the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is in the set, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Consider the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Each term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. So the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> counts exactly the number of ways to write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> precisely when this coefficient is positive, and its value is the number of representations of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> as such a sum.</p>\n<p>The algorithm computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> and reads off its coefficients: the positive-coefficient exponents give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and each coefficient gives the corresponding count.</p>\n<p>Runtime: both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> have degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so their product can be computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> using the FFT-based multiplication seen in class; building the polynomials and scanning the coefficients is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> total.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Multiplying the two indicator polynomials adds every exponent of one to every exponent of the other, so it counts sums for free.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#a": "<p>True. This is precisely the strong duality theorem.</p>\n<p>If the primal polyhedron is bounded and non-empty, then the primal is feasible and its objective is bounded over the feasible region, so the primal has a finite optimum. By the strong duality theorem, whenever the primal is feasible and bounded the dual is also feasible and both problems have the same optimal objective value. Indeed, if the dual were infeasible then by weak duality the primal would be unbounded, which contradicts boundedness. Hence the primal and dual optima coincide, and the statement is true.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A bounded, non-empty primal pins down a finite optimum, and strong duality guarantees the dual meets it exactly with no gap.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#b": "<p>True. A single pivoting step runs in polynomial time in the input length.</p>\n<p>As seen in class, one pivoting step consists of a bounded amount of work. It computes the reduced cost vector and selects a coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to bring into the basis; it runs the ratio test to select a coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> to remove from the basis; and it updates the basis using some matrix inversions and matrix multiplications. Each of these is a standard linear-algebra operation on matrices and vectors whose dimensions are at most the number of variables and constraints, so each runs in time polynomial in the input length. A step performs only a constant number of such operations, so the whole pivoting step is polynomial. The statement is therefore true.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One pivot is just a bit of linear algebra on the current basis; it is the possible number of pivots, not the cost of one, that is the real concern.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#c": "<p>False. No nonzero polynomial can vanish on every integer assignment, by the Schwartz–Zippel lemma.</p>\n<p>Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> is a nonzero polynomial in the five variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> be its total degree. Consider the finite set of integers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, which has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values, and choose each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> uniformly and independently from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. By the Schwartz–Zippel lemma,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSince this probability is strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, there must exist some choice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. These are integer values, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> does not vanish on every integer assignment. Hence no such nonzero polynomial exists and the statement is false.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A nonzero low-degree polynomial has only \"few\" roots, so a large enough grid of integers always contains a point where it is nonzero.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#d": "<p>True. This is exactly the spectral mixing bound for random walks proved in class.</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the normalized adjacency (transition) matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> be the starting distribution, the indicator vector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>. After <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps the distribution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is regular, the all-ones direction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> is the uniform stationary distribution and is fixed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> (its eigenvalue is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> as its uniform component plus a part orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>. Every eigenvalue other than the top one has absolute value at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> keeps the uniform part unchanged and shrinks the orthogonal part by a factor of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for a suitable constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is extremely small, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhere the error vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> has norm below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (in particular below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>).</p>\n<p>The probability that the walk is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>-th entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Hence for every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> the walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so the statement holds.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A big gap between the top eigenvalue and the rest means the walk forgets where it started in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps and spreads out almost uniformly.</p>\n", "2023B-p2#x": "<p><b>Weak duality theorem.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an LP in standard form, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be its dual, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Then for every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p><b>Proof.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> be feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> respectively. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> coordinatewise and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, multiplying the inequality on the left by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup></mrow></math> preserves its direction, giving</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>i.e.</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Now use primal feasibility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> on the left side: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Since these are scalars, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, which is the claim. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>&#x025FB;</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Nonnegativity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> lets the dual inequality survive being multiplied through, and the primal equality collapses the left side to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so every dual value sits below every primal value.</p>\n", "2023B-p3#x": "<p>We generalize the maximal-matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for vertex cover to triples. The algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>While edges remain: pick any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, add all three of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, and erase every edge containing at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This runs in polynomial time, since each round removes at least the picked edge. It returns a valid cover: the loop ends only when no edge is left, and every erased edge had one of its vertices placed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of rounds. Each round adds exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. When an edge is picked, every other edge sharing a vertex with it is erased, so later picks share no vertex with earlier ones; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> picked edges are pairwise vertex-disjoint. Any valid cover must contain a vertex from each picked edge, and since these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> edges share no vertices, that forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> distinct vertices. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>so the algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, use LP rounding: write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, relax to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, solve to get an optimal fractional <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, and round <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Each edge's three values sum to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so one is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and is rounded up, keeping the edge covered; and each rounded-up variable had <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so the cost grows by at most a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The picked edges are disjoint, so the optimum pays at least one vertex each while we pay three, and a triple costs three where a pair cost two.</p>\n", "2023B-p4#a": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> be a Hamiltonian cycle. Define a solution by setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> belongs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. These values are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so the integrality constraint holds.</p>\n<p>Now check the degree constraints. A Hamiltonian cycle is a single closed tour that visits each vertex exactly once, so at every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> it enters through exactly one edge and leaves through exactly one edge. Hence for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> the chosen incoming edges sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>and the chosen outgoing edges sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>All constraints are satisfied, so this indicator vector is a feasible solution (and its objective value equals the total weight of the cycle, which is why the IP models TSP).</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Marking the cycle's edges automatically gives every vertex one edge in and one out, exactly what the constraints demand.</p>\n", "2023B-p4#b": "<p>The converse fails, because the degree constraints only force in-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and out-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at each vertex; they never force the chosen edges to form a single connected tour. So a union of several disjoint cycles is also feasible.</p>\n<p>Concretely, take six vertices with two vertex-disjoint directed triangles, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on these six edges and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> on all others. In each triangle every vertex has exactly one chosen incoming edge and one chosen outgoing edge, so every incoming sum and every outgoing sum equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and all variables are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; the solution is feasible.</p>\n<p>But this is not a Hamiltonian cycle: a Hamiltonian cycle is one cycle passing through all vertices, whereas here the vertices split into two separate cycles and the subgraph is not even connected. So a feasible IP solution need not correspond to a Hamiltonian cycle.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Counting one edge in and one out at each vertex can't tell a single big loop apart from several small ones.</p>\n", "2023B-p4#c": "<p>The added constraints are the subtour-elimination constraints: for every proper subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0228A;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>i.e. the number of chosen edges with both endpoints inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Take any feasible solution. As in part (b), the degree constraints force the chosen edges to form a vertex-disjoint union of directed cycles that together cover all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Suppose, for contradiction, that this is more than one cycle. Pick one of the cycles and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> be its set of vertices; since at least one other cycle exists, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0228A;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> is a proper subset. That cycle uses exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges, all with both endpoints in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>But the subtour constraint for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> requires this sum to be at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a contradiction. So no cycle can close on a proper subset of the vertices. The only remaining possibility is a single cycle through all vertices, that is, a Hamiltonian cycle. Together with part (a), the feasible solutions now correspond exactly to Hamiltonian cycles.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A short loop inside a small set uses one edge too many for that set, so the new cap on internal edges bans every subtour.</p>\n", "2023B-p5#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. We turn reachability into a single matrix power and compute it fast. From class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts the walks of length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff such a walk exists. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, the adjacency matrix of the graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> obtained by adding a self-loop at every vertex. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a path from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> of length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>: a self-loop lets us pad any shorter walk out to length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> can reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> at all, it can along a simple path of length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so reachability is captured exactly by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> by repeated squaring: about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> squarings, each one a fast matrix multiplication.</li>\n<li>Return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correctness holds because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. Running time: forming <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> and reading off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> each cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the power needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> matrix multiplications at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> each, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> total. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, this is asymptotically below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Reachability is just \"is any power entry positive\", and repeated squaring plus fast matrix multiplication computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> under the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> barrier.</p>\n", "2023B-p6#x": "<p>The algorithm is as simple as possible: color each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> independently, uniformly at random red or blue. We show that with high probability this random coloring makes every set have small discrepancy, using the Chernoff bound on each set and a union bound over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> sets.</p>\n<p>For each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be a random variable equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is colored red and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is colored blue. The colors are chosen uniformly, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and for a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> the discrepancy is exactly</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>red&#x000A0;in&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>blue&#x000A0;in&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Fix one set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; note <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, so exceeding the larger threshold implies exceeding the smaller one. Thus, applying the Chernoff bound to the sum of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>By picking the absolute constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> large enough (it does not depend on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>), this probability drops below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; the tail shrinks fast as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> grows.</p>\n<p>Finally, take a union bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> sets in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math>. The probability that <em>some</em> set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> is at most</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>So with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> every set satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> with high probability, as required. The algorithm runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time since it just flips one coin per element.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> colors mostly cancel, so each set's imbalance stays around <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, and Chernoff plus a union bound keep all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> sets small at once.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#a": "<p>False. By the strong duality theorem, if a standard-form LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are both feasible, then both have finite optima and those optimal values are equal. So the optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> can never be strictly greater than the optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>To be concrete, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Weak duality always gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>opt</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>opt</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Strong duality upgrades this: once both are feasible, the duality gap is zero and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>opt</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>opt</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Equal values leave no room for a strict inequality, so the statement is false.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Once both sides are feasible, the primal and dual meet at exactly the same value, so neither can beat the other.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#b": "<p>False. Consider the symmetric matrix</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Its determinant is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the hypothesis holds. But its eigenvalues are both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is not PSD (it is in fact negative definite). This gives a symmetric matrix with nonnegative determinant that fails to be PSD, so the statement is false.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The determinant only sees the product of eigenvalues, so two negative eigenvalues cancel in sign and hide a matrix that is the opposite of PSD.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#c": "<p>True. The Gram-Schmidt process finds an orthonormal basis in polynomial time. First, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> are linearly dependent, greedily keep a maximal linearly independent subset of them (each independence check is a rank computation, done in polynomial time); this subset spans the same subspace. Assume now the vectors are independent.</p>\n<p>Define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is chosen exactly so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> is an orthogonal basis of the span. Finally normalize each vector, replacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>, to obtain an orthonormal basis. Each step is a bounded number of inner products and vector subtractions, so the whole computation runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Subtracting from each vector its projection onto the earlier ones makes them mutually orthogonal, and it is all just inner products and subtractions.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#d": "<p>True. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> be the basic feasible solution with basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and its objective value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> be any feasible solution, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in terms of the basis, its cost is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the reduced-cost vector on the nonbasic variables.</p>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is feasible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and by assumption every entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is strictly positive, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>; hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is optimal. For uniqueness: if some coordinate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> were strictly positive, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> would not be optimal. Thus any optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is the unique optimal solution.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Any move off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> has to switch on a nonbasic variable, and with positive reduced costs each such move only makes the cost worse.</p>\n", "2024A-p2#x": "<p>The algorithm estimates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by sampling. On input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> independent, uniformly random assignments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> (the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is chosen below).</li>\n<li>Evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; each evaluation is fast (linear in the size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>Output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the fraction of the sampled assignments that satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To analyze it, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are mutually independent, and since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is uniform over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> assignments, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the output is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>By the Chernoff bound, the probability that the estimate is off by more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>shrinks fast as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> grows. Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> makes this error drop below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> the output satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and each evaluation is fast, the algorithm runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The density is just the chance a random assignment works, so a fraction over enough random tries lands close to it.</p>\n", "2024A-p3#x": "<p>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math> and give every edge an independent uniform weight from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. We bound the failure chance for one pair and then union-bound over all pairs.</p>\n<p>Fix vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be the family of all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> paths, each viewed as its set of edges. The number of edges is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. By the isolation lemma, when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> elements get independent uniform weights from a range of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the minimum-weight member of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is unique except with probability at most</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Call the pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> bad if its lightest path is not unique; then each pair is bad with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> pairs, so by the union bound the probability that some pair is bad is at most</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> no pair is bad, so every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> has a unique minimum-weight path, as required.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One random weighting quietly breaks all the ties at once, and the isolation bound depends only on the number of edges, not on how many paths there are.</p>\n", "2024A-p4#x": "<p>We use one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable per Boolean variable and one per clause; the clause variable is allowed to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> only when at least one literal of the clause is satisfied, and we maximize the total weight of satisfied clauses.</p>\n<p>Variables: for each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> of the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is true. For each clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, intended to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> be the set of variables appearing in positive literals of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> those in negative literals.</p>\n<p>The integer linear program is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The right-hand side counts the satisfied literals of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>: a positive literal on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; a negative literal on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. So the constraint says: if no literal is satisfied the sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; if some literal is satisfied the sum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and permits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in a maximization, an optimal solution sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> whenever it is allowed, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied.</p>\n<p>Therefore every feasible solution corresponds to a real solution of the problem: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> form a truth assignment, and the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals the total weight of the clauses that this assignment satisfies. Maximizing it solves weighted Max-SAT.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Encode truth as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, let each clause \"count\" its satisfied literals, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> turn on only when that count is positive.</p>\n", "2024A-p6#x": "<p>The algorithm: pick values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly at random from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, form the matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and answer \"yes\" if and only if it is invertible (a determinant check, done in polynomial time).</p>\n<p>Correctness rests on one observation: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The entries of this matrix are linear in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and a determinant of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a polynomial of degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Deciding whether an invertible matrix lies in the span is therefore the same as deciding whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is not the zero polynomial.</p>\n<p>If the span contains no invertible matrix, then every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is singular, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, every random evaluation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and the algorithm always correctly says \"no\".</p>\n<p>If the span does contain an invertible matrix, then some assignment makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> nonzero, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a nonzero polynomial of degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By the Schwartz-Zippel lemma, a random assignment from a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. So the algorithm says \"yes\" with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The algorithm never errs on \"no\" instances and errs with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> on \"yes\" instances; repeating it a few times and answering \"yes\" if any run does makes the error shrink fast, below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> \"Some combination is invertible\" means the determinant polynomial is not identically zero, and a random point almost never lands on a nonzero polynomial's roots.</p>\n", "2024B-p1#a": "<p>True. Integer linear programming is NP-hard, because many NP-complete problems can be encoded as integer linear programs. For example, we saw in class a polynomial-time reduction from the maximum independent set problem to solving integer linear programs: given a graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for each vertex, add the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the optimum equals the size of the maximum independent set.</p>\n<p>So a polynomial-time algorithm for integer linear programs would solve maximum independent set — an NP-complete problem — in polynomial time. Since an NP-complete problem would then be solvable in polynomial time, this would imply P=NP.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> ILP is NP-hard (independent set reduces to it), so a fast ILP solver would collapse P and NP.</p>\n", "2024B-p1#b": "<p>True. Suppose the polytope is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Given the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, first check feasibility, which is easy: verify <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. If either fails, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is not a vertex.</p>\n<p>We saw in class that a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex if and only if the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> indexed by the coordinates where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is strictly positive are linearly independent — that is, the submatrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, whose columns are exactly those coordinates, has full column rank. We can check this in polynomial time by computing the rank of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> (for example by Gaussian elimination) and comparing it to the number of positive coordinates of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. All steps are polynomial, so the test is efficient.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A feasible point is a vertex exactly when the columns for its positive entries are independent, and checking rank is a fast linear-algebra computation.</p>\n", "2024B-p1#d": "<p>True. The expression <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th elementary symmetric polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and elementary symmetric polynomials of the eigenvalues are (up to sign) the coefficients of the characteristic polynomial.</p>\n<p>Indeed, the characteristic polynomial of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so our expression <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> times the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in the characteristic polynomial of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. We saw in class an efficient algorithm that, given a matrix, computes all the coefficients of its characteristic polynomial. Running it on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, we read off the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, multiply by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and obtain the desired value efficiently — with no need to compute the eigenvalues themselves.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> That sum is exactly one coefficient of the characteristic polynomial (up to sign), and we already know how to compute all of those coefficients fast.</p>\n", "2024B-p2#x": "<p>We compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> by a recursive divide-and-conquer, just like FFT: split <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> in half, make two half-size transforms, and combine them with one addition and one subtraction. The running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> are the two halves, each of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Using the recursive block structure of the matrix,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Only the two products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> appear, and each is a Walsh–Hadamard transform of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So we recursively compute those two half-size transforms, then form the top half by adding them and the bottom half by subtracting them, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> extra work. The base case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> applies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo></mrow></math> directly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the running time. We make two recursive calls of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to combine, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhich is the same recurrence as merge sort and FFT, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Both halves of the answer reuse the same two half-size transforms, so one add and one subtract finish the job.</p>\n", "2024B-p3#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the set of minimum cuts of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, a graph on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices. Fix any minimum cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As shown in class, a single run of Karger's contraction algorithm returns this particular cut with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and this holds for every minimum cut, since the analysis never used anything special about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>A single run outputs exactly one cut, so the events \"the algorithm returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" for distinct cuts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are pairwise disjoint. Therefore the probability of returning some minimum cut is the sum of the individual probabilities, and this sum is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>the&#x000A0;algorithm&#x000A0;returns&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Rearranging gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each minimum cut claims its own disjoint slice of at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> probability, and the slices must fit inside a total of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so there is room for at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> of them.</p>\n", "2024B-p4#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> and set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts walks of length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>; adding the identity lets a walk \"wait\", so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> exactly when there is a walk of length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, that is, when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is strongly connected every distance is finite, and a shortest path repeats no vertex, so the diameter is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Hence the diameter is the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> for which every entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> is positive.</p>\n<p>The algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Precompute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by repeated squaring, storing them.</li>\n<li>Binary-search the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> all-positive: to test a value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, multiply the stored powers to form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> and check whether all entries are positive; if so the diameter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, otherwise it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and recurse on the appropriate half.</li>\n<li>Return that smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, which is the diameter.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Runtime: precomputing the powers is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> matrix multiplications, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with fast matrix multiplication, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The binary search has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps, each reusing stored powers, and each positivity check costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is negligible. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> this is asymptotically below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Powers of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> detect \"reachable within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps\", so binary-search the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> that connects every pair.</p>\n", "2024B-p5#x": "<p>We relax the given ILP and round at the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and solve the LP for an optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Since every integer solution is LP-feasible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Round: set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> be the corresponding set.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is representative. Fix any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Its constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is a sum of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> nonnegative terms, so not all can be below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>; some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> contains an element of every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> this holds since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Summing with weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is a valid representative set of weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> times optimal, found in polynomial time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Since each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> elements, one must carry a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> LP share, so rounding at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> covers it while losing at most a factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2024B-p6#x": "<p>Use the following randomized algorithm: for every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, independently include the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> (and leave it out otherwise). This runs in time polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> pairs and we flip one coin per pair. We claim that, for a suitable constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> the resulting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-balanced.</p>\n<p>Fix a size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> and two subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an edge, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and by linearity of expectation</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> are independent, and the deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> is a constant fraction of a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> independent bits, so by the Chernoff bound the chance of deviating that much from the mean shrinks fast:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>for some absolute constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Now take a union bound over the choices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. The number of ways to pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;size&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>m</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;with&#x000A0;a&#x000A0;bad&#x000A0;edge&#x000A0;count</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Write the exponent as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with the constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> chosen large enough, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> beats <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by enough that this bound is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; and since the exponent only becomes more negative as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> grows, the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> holds for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> in the range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Finally union bound over these sizes (there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> of them):</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;not&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>-balanced</mtext><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> the graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-balanced, as required.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Random edges make every pair of equal-sized sides carry about half its pairs as edges, and Chernoff makes each failure chance tiny enough to survive counting all the subsets at once.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#a": "<p>True. A point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is a vertex if and only if the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> at the coordinates where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is strictly positive are linearly independent, i.e. the submatrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> built from those columns has full column rank. This characterization was proved in class.</p>\n<p>Consequently a vertex is determined by its support, the set of coordinates on which it is positive: if two vertices had the same support <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, both would be the solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></math> having independent columns, so that solution is unique and the two vertices coincide. Each support is a subset of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, and there are only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> subsets of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-element set. Hence there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> possible supports, so at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> vertices.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each vertex is pinned down by which coordinates it turns on, and there are only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> such on/off patterns.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#b": "<p>True. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a nonzero polynomial over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math> of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, which has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> elements. By the Schwartz-Zippel lemma,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Since the probability of landing on a root is strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the probability that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is strictly positive. A positive-probability event over a finite set of points cannot be empty, so there exist concrete values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial has too few roots to vanish on a grid of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#c": "<p>False. The class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains every language, even undecidable ones, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains only decidable languages, so the two classes are not equal.</p>\n<p>Take any language <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> at all, say even an undecidable one. Consider the algorithm that on input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> tosses one fair coin and accepts if it comes up heads. It accepts every input with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so both when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> it meets the accept threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and the reject threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Thus this is a valid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> algorithm for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, and so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains all languages.</p>\n<p>Now take any language <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its machine accepts inputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and others with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. We can decide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> by enumerating all coin-toss sequences, computing the exact acceptance probability, and answering \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>\" iff it is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. This always halts (in exponential time), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> is decidable. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has only decidable languages, whereas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has undecidable ones, so they differ.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> With no gap between the accept and reject thresholds, a pure coin flip \"solves\" anything, so that class is uselessly huge.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#d": "<p>True. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> biadjacency matrix of the bipartite graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if left vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is joined to right vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. The number of perfect matchings of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>since each permutation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> with all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is exactly a perfect matching. The determinant is the same sum but with signs:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>sgn</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Working modulo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so all the signs disappear and the two sums are equal mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> equals the number of perfect matchings mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>; it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when that number is odd. The determinant is computable in polynomial time (for example by Gaussian elimination), so the algorithm that computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and outputs it mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> efficiently decides whether the number of perfect matchings in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is odd.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> the pesky signs in the determinant vanish, so the easy-to-compute determinant equals the hard-to-compute matching count.</p>\n", "2024C-p2#x": "<p>We compute the boolean product by casting it to ordinary integer matrix multiplication and using fast matrix multiplication.</p>\n<p>Observe that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x022C1;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02227;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when there is some index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> with both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. If we treat the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> entries as ordinary integers and form the usual product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts exactly those indices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> over the integers using fast matrix multiplication.</li>\n<li>Output the matrix whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> entry is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Runtime: step 1 takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> is the fast matrix-multiplication exponent, and step 2 thresholds each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> entries in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is asymptotically smaller than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> An OR of ANDs is nonzero exactly when the integer dot product is positive, so one fast integer multiply then a \"is it <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>?\" test does the job below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2024C-p3#x": "<p>The algorithm picks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> uniformly at random: set each coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. We show it succeeds with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> using a Chernoff bound on one fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> followed by a union bound over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Fix a vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. For each coordinate let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is fixed and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a fair coin, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The Hamming distance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so by linearity of expectation\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, so Chernoff applies. Having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> falls at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> below its mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></div>\nfor some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the error shrinks fast.</p>\n<p>The algorithm fails only if some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is too close. By the union bound,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>fail</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is swamped by the exponentially small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so for large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> this is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Hence the random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> sits at expected distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> from any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, and getting close is so unlikely that even <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> vectors can't spoil it.</p>\n", "2024C-p4#x": "<p>We never need more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bins (in the worst case one item per bin), so index the possible bins by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Use two kinds of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is used; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is placed in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. The integer program is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts the used bins, which we minimize. The first constraint puts every item in exactly one bin. The second constraint does two jobs at once: whenever bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is used (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) its total weight is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; and if any item is placed in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, the left side is positive, which forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> so the bin is counted (and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> the right side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, forbidding any item there since all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>).</p>\n<p>So every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> solution assigns each item to exactly one bin, respects every used bin's capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and has objective equal to the number of nonempty bins — that is exactly a valid packing and its cost. Conversely any real packing gives a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of the same cost. Therefore minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives the fewest bins.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One variable says \"bin open,\" another says \"item goes here,\" and tying capacity to the open flag makes every feasible point a genuine packing.</p>\n", "2024C-p6#x": "<p>We show that if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is infeasible and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded. The idea is to use Farkas' Lemma to turn the infeasibility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> into a concrete direction, and then walk from the given feasible dual point along that direction: feasibility is preserved while the dual objective runs off to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> be a feasible solution for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is infeasible, Farkas' Lemma gives a vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> such that</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, consider the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>. We claim <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math> is feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Indeed,</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>where the inequality holds because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> by feasibility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, and because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> transposed) together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Now look at the objective value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is positive and grows without bound as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math> while every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math> stays feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded, as claimed.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Farkas hands you a feasible direction that points straight uphill for the dual, so you can keep sliding along it forever and the objective never stops climbing.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#a": "<p>True. We use the averaging (probabilistic) argument: the average number of satisfied clauses over all assignments equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so some assignment satisfies at most that many.</p>\n<p>Pick an assignment at random by setting each variable to true or false independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. A single clause has three literals; it is unsatisfied only when all three literals are false, which happens with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. So each clause is satisfied with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>For clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is satisfied, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the number of satisfied clauses. By linearity of expectation\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nA random quantity cannot always be strictly above its own average, so there must exist at least one assignment that satisfies at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses. Hence the statement is true.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> On average <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> of the clauses are satisfied, so some assignment must land at or below that average.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#b": "<p>True. We compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> by repeated squaring, using an FFT-based multiplication for each squaring, and the total cost is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is a power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, we reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> through the chain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, squaring the current polynomial each time. This is only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> multiplications.</p>\n<p>In the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th squaring we multiply a polynomial of degree about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> by itself. Multiplying two polynomials of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> with FFT costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so this step costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Summing over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, and using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe last sum is a geometric series bounded by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. So the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> are computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> operations, and the statement is true.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Squaring reaches the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th power in only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> multiplications, and the final, largest one already costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#c": "<p>False. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> can be computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> using fast matrix multiplication, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> is not required.</p>\n<p>Stack the vectors as the columns of a matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Then the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix whose columns are exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. So computing all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products is the same as one matrix–matrix multiplication.</p>\n<p>We saw in class that matrix multiplication can be done in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (for example Strassen already gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.81</mn></mrow></math>). This applies to any matrices and never uses the assumption that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> are linearly independent.</p>\n<p>Hence the batch of products is computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, so the claim that it requires time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> is false.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Many matrix–vector products with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> are just one matrix–matrix product, which fast multiplication does below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#d": "<p>True. The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is exactly the eigenspace of eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and for a connected regular graph this eigenvalue has multiplicity exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the space has dimension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The dimension is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: the eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> really occurs. The all-ones vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, because in the (normalized) adjacency matrix of a regular graph every row sums to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> lies in the space and the space is nonzero.</p>\n<p>The dimension is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: we proved in class that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is the largest eigenvalue, and it was shown in the homework that when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected the second-largest eigenvalue is strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has multiplicity exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so no eigenvector for eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is independent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Therefore the eigenspace of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is the single line spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> has dimension exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The statement is true.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Connectivity is exactly what prevents a second independent direction fixed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>; only the all-ones direction survives.</p>\n", "2025A-p2#a": "<p>The dual is <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>We first put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> in the standard equality form the dual recipe uses. Multiply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, then add slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> to reach equalities:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>The recipe: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;free</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. The two blocks of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, the first block is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, so the dual is</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Finally set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, giving the clean form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> above. Note <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the mirror image of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric the dual comes back looking almost exactly like the primal, so the LP is essentially self-dual.</p>\n", "2025A-p2#b": "<p>Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. We show it is feasible for both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with equal objective value, which forces optimality.</p>\n<p>Feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: the primal needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is given.</p>\n<p>Feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: recall <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>; from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is dual-feasible too.</p>\n<p>Equal objective values: the primal value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, and the dual value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. By weak duality every primal-feasible value is at least every dual-feasible value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Here a feasible primal point and a feasible dual point attain the same value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, so neither can be improved past it. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> attains the primal minimum and is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (and by the same token <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>).</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The equality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> satisfies both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> at once, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> plays both roles and pins the two objectives together.</p>\n", "2025A-p3#x": "<p>For each job <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, introduce a boolean variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means job <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is assigned to machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Add one more variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> representing the makespan (the load of the machine that finishes last), which we minimize. The program is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>minimize</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&</mi><mi>T</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The first set of constraints says each job is assigned to exactly one machine. The second set says the load of every machine, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Since we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> subject to it being an upper bound on all machine loads, at the optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> equals the maximum machine load, so the optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is exactly the minimum makespan.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> \"Minimize the maximum load\" is not linear on its own, so we introduce one ceiling variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> above every load and just push it down.</p>\n", "2025A-p4#x": "<p>The algorithm picks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly at random from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> and outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where we take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We show this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>-unbiased with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Fix a nonempty subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. For a uniformly random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a product of independent uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bits, so it is itself uniform on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and has mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, the Chernoff bound gives, for some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> large enough that this bound falls below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. There are fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> nonempty subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, so by a union bound the probability that any single <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> violates the deviation bound is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, which is far below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math>. Hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></math>, every nonempty <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>-unbiased. Sampling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> vectors of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bits takes polynomial time.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each subset-parity is a fair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> coin, so its average over many samples concentrates near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and the error shrinks fast enough to survive a union bound over all subsets.</p>\n", "2025A-p5#x": "<p>We test the polynomial identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by evaluating both determinants at a random point (Schwartz–Zippel).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>For each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, pick an independent uniformly random integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> into both symbolic adjacency matrices.</li>\n<li>Compute the two numeric determinants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, for instance by Gaussian elimination.</li>\n<li>Declare that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are determinantally equivalent if and only if the two numbers are equal.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The algorithm is efficient, since it only computes two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> determinants. For correctness, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the symbolic adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and consider the polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> in the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. A determinant is a sum of products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> entries, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are determinantally equivalent, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> as polynomials, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; then for any choice of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> the two evaluations are equal and the algorithm answers correctly with no error. If they are not equivalent, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and by the Schwartz–Zippel lemma the probability that a nonzero polynomial of degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vanishes at a uniformly random point over a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>. So the algorithm wrongly answers \"equivalent\" with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>, and otherwise correctly answers \"not equivalent.\" The error probability is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Two determinants are equal as polynomials exactly when their difference is the zero polynomial, and a nonzero low-degree polynomial almost never vanishes at a random point.</p>\n", "2025B-p1#a": "<p>False. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>&#x02102;</mi></mrow></math> be the roots of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Take any nonzero polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and pick each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math>. By the Schwartz-Zippel lemma,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Since this probability is strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, there must exist some point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So no nonzero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> can vanish on all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>, and the statement is false.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A low-degree polynomial simply doesn't have enough roots to cover every point of a grid this large.</p>\n", "2025B-p1#b": "<p>True. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Split <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> blocks of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Then</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>so computing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> reduces to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> separate products of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices. Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> product costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> operations with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (using fast matrix multiplication), so the total is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>arithmetic operations, which is fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>. So such an algorithm exists.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A wide matrix is just several square ones side by side, so fast square multiplication carries straight over.</p>\n", "2025B-p1#c": "<p>True. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be the grade of a student chosen uniformly at random, so the average grade is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and for any threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> the probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> equals the fraction of students scoring at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Grades are non-negative, so Markov's inequality applies and gives</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>This probability is exactly the fraction of students scoring at least twice the average, so at most half of the students do.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Since grades can't be negative, too many students can't all sit far above the average, or they would drag the average up.</p>\n", "2025B-p1#d": "<p>True. Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> is singular, so there is a nonzero vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> (with eigenvector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>). We saw in class that for a connected regular graph the normalized adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as an eigenvalue if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> The most negative eigenvalue reaching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is the algebraic signature of a graph with no odd cycles, i.e. a bipartite graph.</p>\n", "2025B-p2#a": "<p>We use one binary variable per set. For each subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is included in the cover. We minimize the total weight of chosen sets, and for each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> we require that at least one chosen set contains it, which is a linear \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" constraint over the sets holding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. The program is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Each element constraint guarantees that some chosen set covers element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, so a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is a valid cover and its objective is that cover's weight; an optimal solution is a minimum-weight set cover.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Switch on sets at cheapest total weight so that every element is hit at least once.</p>\n", "2025B-p2#b": "<p>We relax the ILP from (a) and round at the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and solve the LP for an optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Since every integer cover is LP-feasible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Round: include <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the cover exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>; call the resulting vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The output is a valid cover. Fix an element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. It appears in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets, and the LP constraint gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A sum of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> nonnegative terms reaching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> must have some term at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>, so some set containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and is taken; thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is covered.</p>\n<p>The weight is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> this holds since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Summing with weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The LP is solvable in polynomial time, so this is a polynomial-time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> With only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets over an element, one must carry a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> share, so rounding at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> still covers it while losing at most a factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025B-p3#x": "<p>Split the variables into basic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> and nonbasic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is a basic feasible solution with basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, and as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhich holds at every feasible point. Plugging this into the objective,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhere <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> is exactly the reduced cost vector.</p>\n<p>At the basic feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> the nonbasic variables vanish, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Now let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> be any feasible solution; the same identity applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and by assumption <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and therefore\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> for every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, which means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is an optimal solution.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Rewriting the objective, its value at any feasible point is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>'s value plus a nonnegative reduced-cost term, so nothing can do better than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025B-p4#x": "<p>The idea is to move to point-value form at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity, where dividing polynomials is trivial. The algorithm is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Interpolate the unique polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> whose value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correctness. By assumption <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, so every division in step 2 is well-defined. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So step 2 produces exactly the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> at the roots of unity. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, a polynomial of degree less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is uniquely determined by its values at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> distinct points, so the interpolated polynomial in step 3 is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Runtime. Steps 1 and 3 are a DFT and an inverse DFT, each computed by the FFT in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Step 2 is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> divisions, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. The total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Polynomial division is painful with coefficients but effortless at sample points, and the FFT lets us hop to and from those sample points cheaply.</p>\n", "2025B-p5#x": "<p>The algorithm samples many random colorings and reports the fraction that are legal:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> independent, uniformly random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-colorings <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> (each vertex colored uniformly and independently), where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is chosen below.</li>\n<li>Check each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for legality by scanning all edges (linear time).</li>\n<li>Output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the fraction of the sampled colorings that are legal, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is defined below.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correctness. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a legal coloring. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-coloring, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are mutually independent. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the output is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. By the Chernoff bound,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nbecause the deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> enters the exponent squared.</p>\n<p>Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the failure probability drops below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Thus with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> the output is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Sampling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> colorings and checking each in linear time is polynomial, so the algorithm is efficient.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A random coloring is legal with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the observed legal-fraction estimates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and enough samples make that estimate sharp with overwhelming probability.</p>\n", "2025C-p1#a": "<p>True. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> for some natural number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> be a root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nso <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which is exactly the statement that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> is a root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> as well.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> raised to a power, which stays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025C-p1#b": "<p>True. To bound the minimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math> from above, it is enough to exhibit one method that multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> matrices using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> multiplications.</p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> be two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> matrices. Partition each into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> blocks, where every block is itself a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrix; so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrices whose entries are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> blocks. Multiply these two block matrices using the optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> scheme, which uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> block multiplications. Each such block multiplication is a multiplication of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrices, which can be done with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> scalar multiplications.</p>\n<p>So the total number of scalar multiplications is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the minimum over all methods, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Treat a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> product as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> grid of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> blocks — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> outer products, each an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>-multiplication, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025C-p1#c": "<p>True. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> be the family of edge-sets that form the cuts of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, over the ground set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>; the weight of a cut is the sum of the weights of its edges. Each edge gets an independent uniform weight from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>By the isolation lemma, when the weights are drawn uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the probability that the minimum-weight member of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> is not unique is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so this probability is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Applying the same argument to the negated weights (maximizing weight is minimizing the negation), the probability that the maximum-weight cut is not unique is also at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>By the union bound, the probability that at least one of these two bad events happens is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, both the minimum-weight cut and the maximum-weight cut are unique.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Fine-grained random weights isolate a single extremal set; there is only a quarter chance of a tie at each end, so half the time both ends are unique.</p>\n", "2025C-p1#d": "<p>False. Being a vertex is neither necessary nor sufficient for optimality. Consider the program \"minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\": the objective is constant, so every feasible point is optimal, including the interior points of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which are not vertices. This breaks the \"only if\" direction — an optimal solution need not be a vertex.</p>\n<p>Conversely, take \"minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\": the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is feasible but has value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, while the optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So a vertex need not be optimal, breaking the \"if\" direction. Both directions of the \"iff\" fail, so the statement is false. (What is actually true is the weaker fact that a feasible bounded LP always has an optimal solution that is a vertex.)</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> With a flat or tilted objective, optima can fill a whole face or sit at the opposite vertex, so \"optimal\" and \"vertex\" are not the same thing.</p>\n", "2025C-p2#a": "<p>The relaxed primal is: minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. We put this in standard equality form and read off its dual, and the dual turns out to be exactly (D).</p>\n\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> incidence matrix with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> be the all-ones vector. Row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> covering constraints are exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, and the relaxed primal is</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>minimize&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>To reach standard form we turn each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> into an equality by subtracting a nonnegative slack vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>. Writing the two variable blocks together,</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>minimize&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>s</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>where the cost vector for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> since the slacks carry no cost.</p>\n\n<p>For a standard-form primal \"minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\", the dual is \"maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>\", with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> unrestricted in sign (one dual variable per equality constraint, here one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> per element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>). Plugging in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the dual is</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>maximize&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>y</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;free</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>The top block <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> has, in row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, the entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the bottom block <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow></mrow></math> says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Now substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> (allowed because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is free). The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the top block becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. This is precisely</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>maximize&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>subject&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>which is exactly (D). Hence (D) is the dual of the LP relaxation.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> One dual variable per element, and its constraints say the total charge packed onto any set must not exceed that set's cost.</p>", "2025C-p2#b": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> be an optimal solution of (D), and let the chosen family be all sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> whose constraint (2) is tight, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. We show this family covers every element. Suppose not, and derive a contradiction with the optimality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Assume the chosen family is not a set cover, so some element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> lies in no chosen set. Every set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> that contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is therefore not chosen, which means its constraint is not tight: it is strict,</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;with&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>(If any such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> had a tight constraint it would be in the family, and then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> would be covered.)</p>\n\n<p>Now nudge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> by raising only the coordinate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>: set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, for a small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Check feasibility for (D). The variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears only in the constraints of sets that contain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and every one of those constraints was strict. So we can pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> small enough that all of them still hold; concretely, take any</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>which is positive because each of those slacks is positive. Every constraint not involving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is unchanged, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is feasible.</p>\n\n<p>But the objective strictly increases: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>. This contradicts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> being optimal for the maximization (D).</p>\n\n<p>Therefore no such uncovered <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> exists: every element is contained in some tight set, so the family of tight sets is a set cover.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> If an element were uncovered, all sets touching it have slack, so we could raise that element's dual value and beat the optimum.</p>", "2025C-p2#c": "<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> is the maximum frequency: every element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> appears in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> of the sets. The algorithm solves the dual (D) and takes the tight sets, and we show its cost is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Solve the dual LP (D) to get an optimal solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the family of sets whose constraint (2) is tight.</li>\n<li>Output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>By part (b), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is a valid set cover, so the algorithm is correct. It runs in polynomial time: solving the LP (D) is polynomial, and checking tightness of each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> constraints is cheap.</p>\n\n<p>Now bound the cost. For every set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> by definition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, so summing over the chosen sets,</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Swap the order of summation, grouping by element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>: the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is counted once for each chosen set that contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Writing that count as the number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>,</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> appears in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets in total, hence in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>; together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> this gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>. Therefore</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>f</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the optimal value of (D), which by LP duality equals the optimum of the primal relaxation, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Finally the relaxation only enlarges the feasible region of the integer program, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, the optimal integral set-cover weight. Combining,</p>\n\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n\n<p>Thus the algorithm returns a set cover of weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, i.e. it is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Each element's dual value gets charged to every chosen set containing it, and that happens at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> times, so we overpay by a factor of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "2025C-p3#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the normalized adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. As shown in class, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is regular and connected, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric with eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> satisfying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (the uniform vector is its eigenvector) and a spectral gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The lazy walk is given by the matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. It has the same eigenvectors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, with eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so every eigenvalue lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>; hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The laziness is exactly what keeps the smallest eigenvalue away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Now run the standard mixing argument. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the indicator vector of the start vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>; after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> steps the distribution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric, its unit eigenvectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> form an orthonormal basis. Writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, the uniform distribution. Subtracting it and using orthonormality,</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msubsup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the error shrinks fast:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Finally, the single coordinate at the target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is within the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> distance of the uniform value:</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Thus after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps the walk is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> Laziness stops the walk from oscillating, so it spreads out to nearly uniform, and uniform already puts about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025C-p4#x": "<p>The two programs are equal iff the polynomials they compute are equal, so we test whether the difference is identically zero using a random evaluation. First bound the degree: each entry of a matrix has degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and each matrix multiplication adds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to the degree, so after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> products the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, all have degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The algorithm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Assign each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> an independent, uniformly random value from the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compute both matrix products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> numerically at this assignment, and read off their <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entries.</li>\n<li>Answer \"equal\" if the two entries are equal, and \"different\" otherwise.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Correctness: if the programs compute the same polynomial then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the two entries always agree, and the test never errs. If they compute different polynomials then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, so by the Schwartz-Zippel lemma the chance that our random point is a root is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Thus the error probability is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Runtime: each run is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> multiplications of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> matrices, which is efficient.</p>\n<hr class=\"ex-div\"><p class=\"ex-intuition\"><b>Intuition.</b> A nonzero low-degree polynomial almost never vanishes at a random point, so plugging in random values and comparing catches any difference with high probability.</p>\n"};
